WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.629
For a few hours in May 2016, for the first
time in more than a century,

00:00:04.629 --> 00:00:08.240
Britain was burning zero coal to generate
electricity.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:09.320
None at all.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:12.240
All the coal-fired power plants
were turned off.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.860
And this here is one of the reasons why.

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:18.450
Welcome to the Griffin Wind Farm in Scotland.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:23.200
68 turbines, more than 150 megawatts of capacity.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:27.820
And the turbines rotate and trim the blades
to track the wind in real time.

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:33.140
At full output, this can power entire cities
just from the wind.

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:34.960
But as the world moves to renewable energy,

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:36.859
we’ve got a little bit of a problem.

00:00:36.859 --> 00:00:42.219
Because coal, oil, gas, and nuclear stations
are basically just giant boilers.

00:00:42.219 --> 00:00:45.090
They take water, they heat it up,
that turns to steam,

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:49.109
the steam gets forced through a massive turbine
which rotates and generates power.

00:00:49.109 --> 00:00:51.739
When I say turbine, I don’t mean like the
blades here,

00:00:51.739 --> 00:00:56.829
I mean hundreds of tonnes of steel rotating
thousands of times a minute.

00:00:56.829 --> 00:00:59.520
And all those turbines, all around the grid,

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:01.030
rotate in sync with each other.

00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:04.970
After gearing, they all move at
50 cycles a second.

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:06.430
60 in America.

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:09.750
All perfectly in time: one slows down, they
all slow down.

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:12.030
One speeds up, they all speed up.

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:16.560
And that is what stops your lights
from constantly flickering.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:20.230
Because electricity supply has to always match
demand, pretty much exactly.

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:25.590
There isn't a battery in the world big enough
to store power on a national scale.

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:28.720
So if a power station suddenly falls off the
grid for some reason,

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.980
then where’s that supply going to come from?

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:35.040
And the answer is the kinetic energy that’s
already in those turbines,

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.010
in that spinning thousands of tonnes of steel
around the country.

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:42.710
Homes and factories will literally suck the
kinetic energy out of that turbine

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:45.291
to cover the gap, starting to slow it down.

00:01:45.291 --> 00:01:48.100
But that won’t cause a problem
for 60 seconds or so,

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:51.410
and in that time the National Grid
Control Centre will, I don’t know,

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:53.180
throw another nuclear rod on the barbie

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:57.320
and up the power output from other stations.
They'll cover the demand.

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:01.860
But, well, that relies on
having enough rotational mass,

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:04.240
enough system inertia, as it’s called.

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:05.630
And here, in this turbine?

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:06.630
Well, you can see.

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:08.899
There’s no hundreds of tonnes
of spinning steel,

00:02:08.899 --> 00:02:11.459
just some blades, a bit of metal,

00:02:11.459 --> 00:02:15.680
and some circuitry that feeds the power into
the grid at the right frequency.

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:19.739
If the whole grid were running on
just wind and solar,

00:02:19.739 --> 00:02:22.719
there's no rotational mass.
There's no grid stability.

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:26.030
The minute that supply and demand mismatch,
breakers would trip,

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:28.950
and the whole thing would fall apart.

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:31.269
Now there are some
possible solutions to this.

00:02:31.269 --> 00:02:33.260
Pumped hydro storage
has been around for a while,

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:35.849
which is where you have two lakes
at different heights.

00:02:35.849 --> 00:02:37.769
When you’ve got too much power,
you pump water up,

00:02:37.769 --> 00:02:41.159
and when you’ve got not enough, you drop
it back down again through generators.

00:02:41.159 --> 00:02:43.680
But that takes seconds or minutes
to kick in.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:48.420
There’s also some experiments going on with
things like molten glass storage,

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:50.909
compressed air storage, and flywheels,

00:02:50.909 --> 00:02:54.519
but they’re all experimental at the minute
and not particularly efficient.

00:02:54.519 --> 00:02:57.889
The actual solution may come from somewhere
a little unexpected.

00:02:57.889 --> 00:03:01.780
Because yes, wind is replacing coal and oil,

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:05.890
but it’s also replacing petrol, the gasoline
that you put in your car.

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:08.599
And as more and more and electric cars
get connected to the grid, well,

00:03:08.599 --> 00:03:13.769
you’ve got a load of quite large batteries
with computers.

00:03:13.769 --> 00:03:18.049
Your car could sell power
back to the grid in real-time,

00:03:18.049 --> 00:03:19.989
second by second, as it’s needed.

00:03:19.989 --> 00:03:23.470
Combine that with smart appliances and meters
that can watch the grid

00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:26.680
and work out when would be the best time to
turn on your air-conditioning and fridge is,

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:29.279
and you have a grid that does
a lot of the balancing itself.

00:03:29.279 --> 00:03:32.799
Yes, we will probably always need a
turbine somewhere,

00:03:32.799 --> 00:03:37.560
some big spinning mass of steel to cover
the little glitches second to second,

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:41.340
but the solution to balancing a power grid
is not what we thought it was 20 years ago.

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:44.189
It’s not big, monolithic batteries.

00:03:44.189 --> 00:03:47.409
It’s millions of small ones
up and down the country.

00:03:47.409 --> 00:03:50.049
Thank you very much to all the team from SSE

00:03:50.049 --> 00:03:53.769
who have helped get me, and my team,
up on top of this wind turbine.

00:03:53.769 --> 00:03:55.109
I am incredibly grateful.

00:03:55.109 --> 00:03:57.290
They have gone above and beyond to help out,
so please,

00:03:57.290 --> 00:04:00.029
have a look at the links in the description
for more about them,

00:04:00.029 --> 00:04:01.689
and about the Griffin Wind Farm.

